  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure centered over the lower Great Lakes early this \nmorning, will track east into NY State today. This will provide \ncontinue cool mornings and seasonably warm daytime temperatures \ntoday through Tuesday. Patchy fog will likely occur in typical \nfavored river/sheltered valleys into early this morning and \nagain tonight. A short-wave trough aloft and weak cold front \nwill move across New England on Tue bringing some extra clouds, \nhowever high pressure will become re-established by late Tue.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfternoon Update: Only minor update to include mention of low \nto medium chances for a rumble of thunder Wednesday afternoon \nand evening across northern Herkimer and southern Hamilton \ncounties as thunderstorms could develop during this timeframe. \nThe latest forecast continues to support for conditions to not \nbe favored for severe storms and the current forecast supports \nthe low to medium chances for a rumble of thunder. See previous\ndiscussion for more details.  \n\nPrevious Discussion: After a multi-day stretch of dry weather, \nunsettled conditions are expected Wed into early Thu as a \nprogressive upper level trough and associated frontal system \nmove across the region. Initially on Wed, showers will be mainly\nconfined to areas north/west of Albany associated with the \nsystem's warm front, with showers spreading south/east across \nthe rest of the area Wed night into early Thu as the system's \ncold front sweeps through the area. Will only mention slight \nchance of thunder due to limited instability. Deep layer \nmoisture does increase, with PWAT anomalies forecast to rise to \n+1 to +2 STDEV, so most areas should see at least some rainfall \nalthough flooding is not anticipated due to lack of convection \nand progressive movement of the system. It will turn humid \nbefore a drier air mass starts to build back in behind the cold \nfront on Thu. It will also turn breezy during this time, first \nwith southerly winds on Wed, then westerly winds behind the cold\nfront on Thu with occasional gusts of 20-30 mph.",
